Tracks a custom vector source's in-flight requests separately from the tiles
that want each tile's data. The loader used one registry for both: a tile
being registered meant both that it wanted the data and that the host had been
asked for it. So setTileError dropped the failed tile's registration, and
invalidating the tile found nothing to fetch again
(https://github.com/maplibre/maplibre-native-ffi/issues/782). Keeping the
registration instead stopped a failed refresh from ever retrying, and erasing
it on cancel dropped the other wrapped copies of the tile. Registrations were
also matched by the wrap of the tile's ID, which TilePyramid changes when the
map moves by a world.

Now each canonical tile has subscribers, identified by their actor, and a flag
for whether a fetch is in flight. A tile stays subscribed until it is
destroyed. The loader asks the host for data when a tile needs it, none is
cached, and no fetch is in flight, so a failed tile fetches again when it is
invalidated or needed again. Copies of a tile share one fetch, and the host
hears a cancel once no copy needs the tile. ActorRef gains operator== for the
subscriber identity.
Upstream: not proposed. maplibre/maplibre-native#4928 took the earlier approach
and was closed.

